Standard Input
===============================================================================

The CLI standard-input context exposes one read-only input snapshot through
Unicode text and raw byte views.

```mech
+> @in := cli/stdin
text := @in/text
bytes := @in/bytes
```

1. Snapshot semantics
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The text path returns exact UTF-8 text, including empty input, line endings and
combining characters. The bytes path returns a canonical U8 row matrix of shape
1,N, including 1,0 for empty input. All byte values are preserved without decoding.

The standard backend acquires raw stdin through EOF once. Both views reuse that
snapshot. Invalid UTF-8 is an error only for the text view: it does not prevent
later byte reads. Input acquisition failures are retained for both views. There
is no lossy conversion or numeric parsing.

Each provider retains its first result, including an error. Later reads reuse
that snapshot and do not consume stdin again. A new provider starts a new
snapshot. Reads return the complete stream rather than one line at a time.

2. Planning and capabilities
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Planning checks the context and exact text or bytes path. It supplies a String
placeholder for text and an empty U8 row with dynamic width for bytes.
It never reads stdin or waits for EOF. No input driver is required. Assignments
and sends to stdin are rejected.

Binding +> @in := cli/stdin grants no authority. Standard-input reads are excluded
from default CLI capabilities. Explicitly grant the existing read capability:

```mech
config := {
  run: {
    grants: [{ target: "cli/stdin" operations: ["read"] paths: ["text" "bytes"] }]
  }
}
```

Granting text does not grant bytes. Configured CLI aliases use their instance
target, such as term/stdin. The
experimental native Sequence compiler lowers the default CLI context to a
SnapshotTextRead observation. It retains the exact canonical cli://cli/stdin,
stdin context, text path and read intent in its resource requirements; its
launcher chooses the existing secure or trusted host policy.

3. Embedding
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Custom raw backends implement stdin_bytes() and return Some(result) for a real
raw acquisition, including failure. The provider derives text from those bytes
and never calls stdin_text() on that route. None means a legacy text-only backend;
it must not consume input. Such a backend can retain stdin_text() support, while
byte reads fail explicitly without synthesizing a byte stream from a String.
The provider caches acquisition and view results independently. No new stream
size or input-consumption defaults are introduced by the byte paths.
